From: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: qcom-cci: let the controller idle after probe

cci_probe() leaves the device runtime-ACTIVE and never balances it:

	pm_runtime_set_autosuspend_delay(dev, MSEC_PER_SEC);
	pm_runtime_use_autosuspend(dev);
	pm_runtime_set_active(dev);
	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
	...
	return 0;

pm_runtime_set_active() only declares the state; the autosuspend timer is
armed by a put, and the sole get/put pair lives in cci_xfer(). On a system
that never performs a camera transfer nothing ever puts, so the runtime
suspend callback -- and with it cci_disable_clocks() -- is never reached and
the CCI clocks stay on for the lifetime of the system.

On sdm845 that pins cam_cc_pll0 and cam_cc_slow_ahb_clk_src, both direct
children of bi_tcxo, which holds the XO vote up and prevents the SoC from
reaching AOSD/CXSD sleep. Measured on a OnePlus 6 (enchilada): three of the
six bi_tcxo references were owned by CCI with no camera in use.

Drop an idle at the end of probe so the normal autosuspend path runs.

Signed-off-by: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
---
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
@@ -629,6 +629,12 @@
 		}
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Nothing has taken a runtime reference; arm the autosuspend timer so
+	 * the clocks enabled by cci_init() are released when idle.
+	 */
+	pm_runtime_idle(dev);
+
 	return 0;
 
 error_i2c:
